    14	
    15	## §1 — Overview
    16	
    17	Chat Mode is the user-driven, conversational orchestration mode. The manager runs **one per-task
    18	slice** per user-typed task and returns control to the user after each slice. There is no upfront
    19	task list; the session's shape is the union of all per-task slices the user types between session
    20	start and an explicit end-of-session signal.
    21	
    22	**Why this doc exists.** The global orchestration mode contract in `orchestration/SKILL.md` (lines
    23	241–242) originally stated "Mode controls user gates; it does not relax the workflow." That lock has
    24	been superseded by the mode-dispatched state-machine design ratified in session
    25	`2026-05-28-8eed14fb`. Chat Mode now controls *which state machine runs*, not just gate density —
    26	it dispatches a per-task slice loop, not the linear Ideation → Preparation → Planning → Execution
    27	→ Wrap-up sequence. See the CORRECTION annotation in `orchestration/SKILL.md §
    28	Orchestration Mode` for the ADR record.
    29	
    30	---
    31	
    32	## §2 — Mode posture
    33	
    34	Chat Mode is the user-driven, conversational orchestration mode. **The user types one task at a
    35	time; the manager runs a full per-task slice and returns to the user.** No upfront task list. No
    36	autonomous multi-task runway. The session's shape is the union of all per-task slices the user
    37	types between session start and the user's explicit end-of-session signal.
    38	
    39	**Term lock — "per-task slice."** The single canonical term for the unit Chat dispatches is
    40	**per-task slice**. Synonyms ("per-user-typed-task slice", "task slice", "Chat task") are
    41	non-canonical — use "per-task slice" consistently in all downstream prose.
    42	
    43	This re-frames the 6-step state machine:
    44	
    45	- **Configuration** (Step 1) runs once per session — unchanged.
    46	- **Wrap-up** (Step 6) runs once per session, triggered only on explicit user signal — see §7.
    47	- Between them, the manager runs **per-task slices**, not a linear Ideation → Preparation →
    48	  Planning → Execution → Wrap-up sequence.
    49	- Each per-task slice contains its own Ideation loop, its own Preparation loop (which resolves to
    50	  `state: Skipped` at loop entry per R1), its own mini Planning loop, its own mini Execution loop,
    51	  and its own task-record boundary.
    52	
    53	This is the structural change that supersedes the original SKILL.md 241–242 lock. Mode no longer
    54	just controls whether the manager pauses for `AskUserQuestion`; mode now controls which state
    55	machine runs between Configuration and Wrap-up.
    56	
