   209	
   210	## §6 — task-record artifact spec
   211	
   212	Per Chat task, the per-task slice's MEMORIZATION assistant writes one `task-record.md` at the
   213	task boundary (after the mini Execution Loop's last sub-step exits, or after mini Planning if
   214	mini Execution was skipped); the manager verifies its presence before the user review gate. See
   215	§6.4 for owner details.
   216	
   217	### 6.1 Path and layout
   218	
   219	**Decision D-A (session-local only):** the task-record is session-scope only. It is NOT a
   220	project-memory type, NOT a feature-memory entry, and NOT routed to `notes/` (project-level
   221	`notes/` is a session-journal type per `memorization/rules.md`). It lives under the session tree:
   222	
   223	```
   224	sessions/{date}-{session-id}/chat/tasks/{NN}-{slug}/task-record.md
   225	```
   226	
   227	where:
   228	
   229	- `{date}` — session start date `YYYY-MM-DD`
   230	- `{session-id}` — session UUID
   231	- `{NN}` — zero-padded ordinal within the session (chronological aid only)
   232	- `{slug}` — subject-descriptive kebab-case slug per `memorization/rules.md § 1.3`; names the
   233	  task's deliverable concept, NOT the ordinal (e.g., `03-chat-mode-redesign-idea-doc`, not
   234	  `03-task-3`)
   235	
   236	**Decision D-B (per-task slice layout):** the full per-task slice session layout is:
   237	
   238	```
   239	sessions/{date}-{ssid}/chat/tasks/{NN}-{slug}/
   240	    task-record.md
   241	    ideation/
   242	        {rawdata,staging,artifacts,evaluation}/
   243	    planning/
   244	        {rawdata,staging,artifacts,evaluation}/
   245	    execution/
   246	        {rawdata,staging,artifacts,evaluation}/
   247	```
   248	
   249	Preparation is not present in the directory tree for tasks where `state: Skipped` (the default
   250	per R1). If a user opts into Preparation for a specific task, a `preparation/` subdirectory
   251	appears with the same quartet.
   252	
   253	### 6.2 Frontmatter type — deferred to Planning
   254	
   255	**Frontmatter type for `task-record.md` is deferred.** The iter1 ideation draft proposed `type:
   256	notes` but that collides with the project-level chronological journal convention in
   257	`memorization/templates/notes.md` (project-only type, one entry per session — see
   258	`mistakes/prose-reclassification-target-is-project-level-notes.md`). Planning will resolve this
   259	by choosing one of:
   260	
   261	(a) `artifact_type: task-record` aligned to the `artifacts/` schema in `memorization/SKILL.md §
   262	Artifact frontmatter schema`, or
   263	
   264	(b) a new dedicated `task-record` template under `memorization/templates/task-record.md`.
   265	
   266	Either way the per-task record is **session-scope only** (D-A). Until Planning makes this choice,
   267	agents authoring a task-record should use the `artifacts/` frontmatter schema as a default
   268	(carrying `loop`, `iter`, `artifact_type: task-record`, `created_at`, `status`).
   269	
   270	### 6.3 Body structure
   271	
   272	5–10 lines, five sections:
   273	
   274	```markdown
   275	## What the user asked
   276	(1–2 lines — verbatim or paraphrased ask; prefer paraphrase if the ask contains secrets or PII)
   277	
   278	## What shipped
   279	(1–3 lines — the concrete deliverable + path)
   280	
   281	## Decisions taken
   282	(1–3 lines — citing decisions staged during the per-task slice)
   283	
   284	## Open threads
   285	(1–2 lines — anything deferred or flagged)
   286	
   287	## Pointers
   288	(1–2 lines — paths to the per-task slice's artifacts/ files)
   289	```
   290	
   291	### 6.4 Writer
   292	
   293	The task-record is written by:
   294	
   295	- The mini Execution Loop's MEMORIZATION assistant on PASS of the last sub-step, OR
   296	- The mini Planning Loop's MEMORIZATION assistant on PASS, if mini Execution was skipped.
   297	
   298	Either way it is the **assistant role**. The manager verifies presence of the task-record at the
   299	user review gate before presenting `AskUserQuestion` options.
   300	
   301	### 6.5 Wrap-up role
   302	
   303	Wrap-up's MEMORIZATION reads every `task-record.md` in the session (path pattern:
   304	`chat/tasks/*/task-record.md`), plus the session transcript, and consolidates into project-memory
   305	promotions per `wrap-up/SKILL.md`. Wrap-up may reclassify task-record body narrative into
   306	project-level `notes/` per `mistakes/prose-reclassification-target-is-project-level-notes.md`
   307	(project-tier, not feature-tier).
   308	
   309	---
   310	
   311	## §7 — Wrap-up trigger (explicit end-of-session)
   312	
   313	In Chat Mode, Wrap-up runs **only when the user explicitly signals end-of-session**. The signal is
   314	one of:
   315	
   316	- The user selects "Wrap up the session" at the per-task user review gate (AskUserQuestion).
   317	- The user types an explicit end-of-session message (e.g., "we're done", "wrap up", "end
   318	  session").
   319	- The user invokes `/gobbi wrap-up` (if such a command exists; otherwise the message form above).
   320	
   321	The manager does **NOT** auto-trigger Wrap-up on any other signal:
   322	
   323	- No auto-trigger on "no more tasks for now".
   324	- No auto-trigger on idle.
   325	- No auto-trigger after N tasks.
   326	
   327	The discipline is symmetric to the Always-Ask Destructive category in
   328	`discussion/SKILL.md § Decision Classification`: ending the session changes durable memory
   329	(mistake promotion, archive moves, handoff write) — that decision is the user's, always.
   330	
   331	**Partial session survival.** If the user closes the session without an explicit Wrap-up signal
   332	(terminal hang-up), the partial session-memory survives: every loop's MEMORIZATION ran (per §4),
   333	transcript + session.json + per-loop artifacts are all written, and the per-task task-records are
   334	written. A future session can resume per the existing 3-state worktree idempotency guard in
   335	`orchestration/SKILL.md § Step 1` row 5.
